# fixedbugs, defect 329, lane b11-misc, 2026-10-05.
#
# SYMPTOM: a letter after the digits a zero opens, `0644u` or `00x7`, was told
# twice: `leading_zero` at the digits, whose note already names what follows
# them, and then the letters as a name after a number, `expected_end_of_line`,
# or inside brackets `expected_args_close` and `expected_separator`; a
# character beyond ASCII was `unexpected_character`.
#
# CAUSE: the refused literal ended at its digits, so whatever joined them was
# scanned again as a token of its own.
#
# FIX: what joins the digits, letters, digits, separators, characters beyond
# ASCII and a fraction once something has joined, is the refused literal's
# (`leading_zeros.joined_end`), told in its note and never again. The two
# shapes of `fixedbugs-324-*` that pinned the second message moved with it.

function main()
    a = 0644u8    #~ leading_zero
    b = 007e3    #~ leading_zero
    c = 0_7u    #~ leading_zero
    d = 00b1    #~ leading_zero
    e = 00x7.5    #~ leading_zero
    f = 0644é    #~ leading_zero
    print(0644u)    #~ leading_zero
    g = [00x7, 1]    #~ leading_zero
    print(a, b, c, d, e, f, g)
